1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,716
Here is a filling puzzle. Each row must use the numbers from one up to its length, exactly

2
00:00:05,716 --> 00:00:11,052
once. Each column must avoid repeats. If we grow this staircase by one row, how many

3
00:00:11,052 --> 00:00:13,656
new fillings can one old filling produce?

4
00:00:14,033 --> 00:00:19,924
Begin with a familiar square. In this three by three grid, every row uses one, two, and

5
00:00:19,924 --> 00:00:24,596
three, and every column does too. Colours help us follow each number.

6
00:00:24,596 --> 00:00:28,049
This is a Latin square, with no extra Sudoku boxes.

7
00:00:28,433 --> 00:00:33,862
Now allow shorter rows. Our running shape has row lengths five, four, three, and three.

8
00:00:33,862 --> 00:00:36,421
The two bottom rows have the same length.

9
00:00:36,421 --> 00:00:41,537
A left-aligned shape filled by our row and column rules is called a Latin tableau.

10
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:45,493
Read the top row: four, five, two, one, three.

11
00:00:45,493 --> 00:00:48,743
It uses each number from one to five once.

12
00:00:48,743 --> 00:00:53,696
The next row uses one to four. The bottom rows use one to three.

13
00:00:53,696 --> 00:00:57,797
Different row lengths mean different allowed numbers.

14
00:00:58,167 --> 00:01:02,115
Now read down the first column: four, three, two, one.

15
00:01:02,115 --> 00:01:06,356
No repeats. The second column reads five, one, three, two.

16
00:01:06,356 --> 00:01:13,302
Again, no repeats. A repeated number would break the rule even when both rows were individually

17
00:01:13,302 --> 00:01:13,887
perfect.

18
00:01:14,267 --> 00:01:16,804
This is an independent verification note.

19
00:01:16,804 --> 00:01:22,126
An earlier public proof, dated the twenty second of October twenty twenty six, already

20
00:01:22,126 --> 00:01:25,592
established the same count and four-extension mechanism.

21
00:01:25,592 --> 00:01:30,419
We are explaining and checking that mathematics, not claiming a new discovery.

22
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:33,389
Place a row of six cells above our example.

23
00:01:33,389 --> 00:01:38,447
The old entries stay fixed. The new row must contain one to six, and each entry must

24
00:01:38,447 --> 00:01:43,686
avoid the numbers already below it. We are counting legal extensions of this particular

25
00:01:43,686 --> 00:01:44,168
filling.

26
00:01:44,567 --> 00:01:48,532
One legal new row is five, four, six, three, one, two.

27
00:01:48,532 --> 00:01:52,277
Follow the five down its column, and then the four.

28
00:01:52,277 --> 00:01:58,079
Neither clashes with an old entry. The six is new, so it cannot clash anywhere.

29
00:01:58,467 --> 00:02:02,953
There are five hundred and seventy six fillings of the six-column shape.

30
00:02:02,953 --> 00:02:07,939
Our proof starts with this entire finite family, not just the example on screen.

31
00:02:07,939 --> 00:02:12,675
The next step is to show that every one of them has exactly four extensions.

32
00:02:13,067 --> 00:02:16,535
Now add a row of seven above this six-column example.

33
00:02:16,535 --> 00:02:22,098
Look at the first three old columns. Each already contains one, two, three, and four.

34
00:02:22,098 --> 00:02:26,483
None of those numbers can go into any of our first three new cells.

35
00:02:26,867 --> 00:02:30,245
So those three cells must contain five, six, and seven.

36
00:02:30,245 --> 00:02:33,746
Five already appears in the first and second old columns.

37
00:02:33,746 --> 00:02:36,940
Only the third column is available for the new five.

38
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:39,275
Put it there. This position is forced.

39
00:02:39,667 --> 00:02:42,371
The old six is also in that third column.

40
00:02:42,371 --> 00:02:45,472
But we have just filled its new cell with five.

41
00:02:45,472 --> 00:02:50,156
The first two new cells are therefore both available for six and seven.

42
00:02:50,156 --> 00:02:53,323
Swap them: two possibilities, with no collision.

43
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:57,525
What about the fourth cell? Below it are three, one, and two.

44
00:02:57,525 --> 00:03:02,917
Those numbers are forbidden. After using five, six, and seven on the left, four is the

45
00:03:02,917 --> 00:03:06,804
largest remaining number. It is the only possible choice here.

46
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:10,715
In a larger staircase, this forced section continues.

47
00:03:10,715 --> 00:03:16,353
The first middle column blocks all smaller remaining values, so the largest remaining

48
00:03:16,353 --> 00:03:19,537
value goes there. The next column does the same.

49
00:03:19,537 --> 00:03:22,920
Values fall into place like a descending staircase.

50
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:29,100
Return to our seven-cell row. The last three positions must contain one, two, and three.

51
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:32,594
Under the first of these positions sit one and three.

52
00:03:32,594 --> 00:03:36,812
So that new cell must be two. Again, the first choice is forced.

53
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:41,764
The next old column contains two, the number we have already placed.

54
00:03:41,764 --> 00:03:46,194
That leaves one and three free to swap across the final two cells.

55
00:03:46,194 --> 00:03:51,899
This is a second independent choice. Swapping here does not disturb the large numbers

56
00:03:51,899 --> 00:03:52,704
on the left.

57
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:56,270
Two choices on the left, and two on the right.

58
00:03:56,270 --> 00:04:00,266
Every left choice can be combined with every right choice.

59
00:04:00,266 --> 00:04:06,123
So this particular old filling produces exactly two times two, or four, legal new top

60
00:04:06,123 --> 00:04:06,468
rows.

61
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:12,497
We have not just found four examples. The column restrictions forced the first block,

62
00:04:12,497 --> 00:04:16,471
forced every middle entry, and forced the first small entry.

63
00:04:16,471 --> 00:04:20,379
Nothing else can vary. That is why four is the entire list.

64
00:04:20,767 --> 00:04:25,888
To make this a proof for every size, we need facts that survive the extension.

65
00:04:25,888 --> 00:04:31,601
Such a fact is called an invariant. Here the paper uses five: three describe row blocks

66
00:04:31,601 --> 00:04:35,935
and column contents; two prevent clashes between the top two rows.

67
00:04:36,333 --> 00:04:41,594
First, the top row always splits into three large numbers, a forced descending middle,

68
00:04:41,594 --> 00:04:47,038
and three small numbers. The middle columns contain exactly the smallest possible values.

69
00:04:47,038 --> 00:04:52,053
In the first three columns, each smaller number has already appeared in all three.

70
00:04:52,433 --> 00:04:58,245
The large-number safeguard says this: the largest entry of the top row is not above the

71
00:04:58,245 --> 00:05:03,924
largest entry of the row below. In our example, six is in column three, while the old

72
00:05:03,924 --> 00:05:05,393
five is in column two.

73
00:05:05,767 --> 00:05:09,128
That safeguard makes both large-number swaps legal.

74
00:05:09,128 --> 00:05:14,994
After adding the new row, the seven is in column one or two, while six is below in column

75
00:05:14,994 --> 00:05:19,279
three. The same safeguard survives, ready for the next extension.

76
00:05:19,667 --> 00:05:25,206
The small-number safeguard compares the last entry of the top row with the last entry

77
00:05:25,206 --> 00:05:29,638
of the row below. They differ. Here those entries are two and three.

78
00:05:29,638 --> 00:05:33,875
This forces the old last entry to equal the missing small number.

79
00:05:34,267 --> 00:05:37,414
After extension, the new last entry is one or three.

80
00:05:37,414 --> 00:05:40,139
The old last entry is two. They still differ.

81
00:05:40,139 --> 00:05:44,800
Meanwhile each forced middle entry completes the next consecutive column set.

82
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:49,219
All five facts are restored, so the construction can repeat indefinitely.

83
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:55,083
The starting check examines all five hundred and seventy six six-column tableaux, and

84
00:05:55,083 --> 00:06:00,502
every one satisfies those five facts. Two different enumeration methods agree on the

85
00:06:00,502 --> 00:06:05,920
count. This finite base case is the only exhaustive computation the induction needs.

86
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:09,847
Imagine repeatedly pressing a four-way growth button.

87
00:06:09,847 --> 00:06:16,004
Five hundred and seventy six becomes two thousand three hundred and four, then nine thousand

88
00:06:16,004 --> 00:06:21,692
two hundred and sixteen. Each old tableau has four children, and deleting the top row

89
00:06:21,692 --> 00:06:23,700
identifies exactly one parent.

90
00:06:24,067 --> 00:06:26,547
That last point prevents double counting.

91
00:06:26,547 --> 00:06:31,628
Two different parents cannot produce the same full tableau, because removing its top

92
00:06:31,628 --> 00:06:37,375
row would have to give both parents. And every larger tableau has a smaller tableau underneath,

93
00:06:37,375 --> 00:06:38,827
so no filling is missed.

94
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:45,571
Starting at size six, the formula is five hundred and seventy six times four to the power

95
00:06:45,571 --> 00:06:51,442
k minus six. The separately checked size-five count is one hundred and forty four.

96
00:06:51,442 --> 00:06:55,952
Together these give nine times four to the power k minus three.

97
00:06:56,333 --> 00:07:02,371
The retained checks reproduce one hundred and forty four, five hundred and seventy six,

98
00:07:02,371 --> 00:07:07,298
and two thousand three hundred and four using both enumeration methods.

99
00:07:07,298 --> 00:07:11,393
They also test the invariants and four extensions directly.

100
00:07:11,393 --> 00:07:16,181
These checks support the argument; they do not replace the induction.

101
00:07:16,567 --> 00:07:21,139
This family also satisfies a broader shape condition called wideness.

102
00:07:21,139 --> 00:07:25,248
Roughly, enough width must remain even after rows are removed.

103
00:07:25,248 --> 00:07:30,814
The general conjecture asks whether that shape condition guarantees a Latin filling.

104
00:07:30,814 --> 00:07:34,327
Our one family does not settle that general question.

105
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:39,776
There are three things to remember. Column exclusions force most of the new row.

106
00:07:39,776 --> 00:07:42,886
Two independent swaps leave exactly four choices.

107
00:07:42,886 --> 00:07:48,787
An invariant carries the same reasoning from a checked finite base to every larger staircase.

108
00:07:48,787 --> 00:07:50,564
That is the proof mechanism.

109
00:07:50,933 --> 00:07:56,695
The count is proved in this note by an ordinary induction and finite checks, with high

110
00:07:56,695 --> 00:08:00,379
correctness confidence. The earlier proof has priority.

111
00:08:00,379 --> 00:08:04,064
No formal build of that earlier work was replayed here.

112
00:08:04,064 --> 00:08:06,341
The wider conjectures remain open.
